        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A belt that will not run smoothly is unsettling in a way a noise is not, because you feel
          it through your feet before you have worked out what it is. The useful thing is that a
          jerk almost always keeps a rhythm, and the rhythm points at the part. The less useful
          thing is that one of the rhythms is no rhythm at all, and that one is a safety problem
          rather than a maintenance one.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Is My Treadmill Belt Jerking?</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill belt that jerks or stutters is usually doing one of three things, and the
            timing tells you which. <strong>A jerk once per revolution of the belt</strong> — every
            few seconds at walking speed, with or without you on it — is the belt or a roller: a
            stiff seam, a damaged section, debris underneath, or a roller with a fault.
            <strong>A stutter with each footstrike</strong> is friction or tension: a dry deck or a
            slack belt letting the belt hesitate under your weight. <strong>Speed that surges or
            fluctuates on its own</strong>, independent of the belt position and your stride, is
            electrical: the speed sensor, the controller board, worn motor brushes or a loose
            connection.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first two are usually owner-fixable or at least owner-diagnosable. The third is not,
            and it is one of the symptoms where the right move is to stop using the machine rather
            than keep investigating. Unexpected acceleration is how people come off the back of
            treadmills, and a controller that cannot hold a steady speed has no business carrying
            someone at pace.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page works through each pattern in turn, starting with the test that
            sorts them, and links out to the neighbouring guides where a cause already has its own
            page rather than repeating it here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sort-by-rhythm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Sort the Jerk by Its Rhythm</h2>
<p>
            You need ten minutes, a strip of painter's tape and someone to press the stop button if
            you are on the machine. Unplug first, put the tape across the belt near one edge, then
            plug back in, stand on the side rails and start the belt at its lowest speed with nobody
            on the running surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Watch the tape come round.</strong> If the belt jerks, hesitates or kicks each
            time the tape reaches the same point — usually as it passes over the front or rear
            roller — the fault travels with the belt. Move the tape until it sits on the jerk and
            you have found the section to inspect. At walking speeds a full belt revolution takes a
            few seconds on a typical home machine, so this rhythm is slow and easy to count.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the empty belt runs smoothly</strong>, step on and walk at 2 to 3 mph. A
            stutter that lands each time a foot lands, and vanishes when you step back onto the
            rails, is a friction or tension problem: the belt is being asked to carry more load than
            the drive can deliver at that instant.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the speed rises and falls with no relationship to the tape or your
            feet</strong> — a surge every so often, a slow hunting up and down, or a change that
            happens while you are standing still on the rails — it is the electronics deciding to
            change speed. That is the pattern that ends the investigation.
          </p>
          <p>
            One complication: a fast, rhythmic shudder rather than a distinct jerk usually comes
            from a part that turns many times per second, such as a roller or the motor. Those
            blur into vibration as speed rises, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-thumping-noise/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">thumping and knocking guide</a>
            covers the same timing technique applied to noise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surging-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Surging on Its Own: Stop Using It First</h2>
<p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            names four symptoms that mean stop immediately rather than diagnose: a burning smell, a
            breaker that trips on start-up, a belt that keeps moving after the safety key is pulled,
            and speed that surges on its own. This page sits on the last of those, and it is worth
            being clear about why it is on the list.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill's controller holds speed by constantly comparing what you asked for with
            what a sensor says the belt is doing, and adjusting the motor to close the gap. When
            that loop goes wrong, the controller can deliver a burst of power it did not intend.
            On a walking pace that is a lurch. On a running pace it can be a step change of speed
            that your legs did not agree to, and the usual outcome of that is a fall backwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            So: stop the belt, take the safety key out, unplug the machine and do not use it until
            the cause is found. Write down what happened — the speed you were at, whether there was
            an error code, whether it surged up or dropped down, whether it coincided with anyone
            switching on something else in the house. That information is what a technician or a
            support line needs, and the sections below explain where it usually leads.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not confuse this with a programmed workout changing speed, which is the commonest
            false alarm. Interval and hill programmes alter speed and incline on a schedule, and some
            heart-rate programmes adjust them in response to a sensor reading. If you were in a
            programme, try the same speed in manual mode before assuming a fault.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="once-per-revolution" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Once Per Revolution: The Belt Itself</h2>
<p>
            If the tape test pins the jerk to one point on the belt, unplug the machine and look at
            that section closely, top and underside. The causes here are mechanical and visible.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A stiff or lifting seam.</strong> Every belt is a strip joined into a loop. An
            old, heat-damaged or poorly made seam stiffens, and a stiff joint bending round a small
            roller produces a kick each time it passes. A ridge you can feel with a fingertip, a
            corner starting to peel or a seam gone hard and glassy are all signs. A new belt can
            show its seam for a few hours and settle; an old one only gets worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delamination or a bulge.</strong> If the running surface is separating from its
            fabric backing, the belt has a soft lump that catches as it passes over the rollers and
            the deck. This is the one belt fault that means stop rather than schedule a repair,
            because a delaminating belt can tear or bunch underfoot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Something stuck to the underside.</strong> A blob of old lubricant, tape, gum
            from a shoe or a wad of compacted fibre forms a lump that drags across the deck and
            catches at the rollers. Slacken the belt, clean it off, and the jerk often vanishes
            completely. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-clean-under-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning under a treadmill belt</a>
            covers getting at it without damaging anything.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A belt at the end of its life.</strong> A belt with a glazed underside, frayed
            edges or a permanent crease can grab and release unevenly round its loop. If several of
            those signs are present, the question is less how to fix the jerk and more whether the
            belt is due, which is what
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often a belt should be replaced</a>
            answers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rollers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Once Per Roller Turn: Rollers, Bearings and the Pulley</h2>
<p>
            Rollers are small in diameter and turn several times for every revolution of the belt,
            so a roller fault rarely produces a single distinct jerk. It produces a faster pulse —
            a rhythmic shudder or snatch you can feel underfoot, which quickens sharply as you raise
            the speed. With the machine running empty at low speed you can sometimes see the cause
            at the roller ends.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A bent roller.</strong> Usually the result of a dropped weight, rough transport
            or the machine being tipped onto its end. A bent roller pushes the belt up and down once
            per turn. It is a replacement part rather than a repair, and worth doing promptly
            because the wobble loads the bearings and the belt with every rotation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A failing bearing.</strong> A worn bearing with play in it, or one that has
            started to bind, makes the roller turn unevenly. It tends to come with a rumble or grind
            from that end of the machine. Consumer rollers usually have sealed bearings pressed in,
            so the fix is generally a replacement roller. The commonest avoidable cause is a belt
            tensioned far past correct, which loads the bearings continuously.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A loose front pulley.</strong> The front roller carries the pulley the motor's
            drive belt runs on. If that pulley has worked loose on its shaft, the drive can slip and
            catch once per turn. That is inside the motor hood and, on most machines still under
            warranty, a support call rather than a screwdriver job.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A rear roller that shifts.</strong> If the rear tension bolts have been backed
            off unevenly or one has come loose, the roller can move slightly in its brackets under
            load. Setting tension correctly and evenly is covered in
            <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how tight a treadmill belt should be</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="with-each-footstrike" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. With Each Footstrike: Friction and Tension</h2>
<p>
            A stutter that arrives with every step, and disappears with the belt running empty, is
            a hesitation under load. The belt is momentarily carried more slowly than the motor
            intends, then catches up. It feels like a jerk because you feel the catch-up as much as
            the hesitation.
          </p>
          <p>
            This symptom has its own full guide, because the diagnosis order matters and the
            commonest first instinct — tightening the belt — is usually wrong. In short: a dry deck
            raises friction until your weight is enough to make the belt slip on the front roller;
            a genuinely slack belt does the same thing for a different reason; and a motor that is
            hot or undersized for the user loses speed under each footstrike. Lubrication comes
            first, tension second, and the two-to-three-inch lift test at the middle of the deck is
            how you know whether tension is actually the problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rather than repeat that procedure here, go to
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt slows down when I step on it</a>,
            which walks through deck, tension, drive belt and motor in order with a marker test that
            separates belt slip from motor slowdown. If the belt also wanders sideways, the related
            guide on
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping and tracking</a>
            covers both together.
          </p>
          <p>
            One overlap worth flagging: a dry patch on the deck, rather than a uniformly dry deck,
            can produce a jerk that looks partly like a once-per-revolution fault, because the belt
            grabs at the same spot under each foot. Lubricating the whole running line, as the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubrication guide</a>
            describes, resolves both.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drive-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Jerks at Start-Up or When Changing Speed</h2>
<p>
            Some machines run smoothly at a steady speed and only snatch when starting or when you
            press the speed buttons. That timing points at the drive system rather than the running
            belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Inside the motor hood, a short ribbed drive belt connects the motor pulley to the front
            roller. When it is worn, glazed or loose, it slips under the extra load of acceleration
            and then grips again, which you feel as a jerk and often hear as a brief squeal from the
            front of the machine. A damaged section on the drive belt can also produce a pulse once
            per drive-belt revolution.
          </p>
          <p>
            The important point is that the rear adjustment bolts do not touch the drive belt at
            all. Owners who mistake one for the other over-tighten the running belt chasing a
            start-up symptom that lives elsewhere, and damage the bearings and running belt in the
            process. If two rounds of running-belt adjustment have not changed a start-up-only jerk,
            stop turning the rear bolts.
          </p>
          <p>
            A related start-up behaviour is normal, not a fault: many machines ramp gently to the
            first speed rather than leaping to it, and some take a moment to respond after a speed
            button is pressed. If a jerk at start-up has always been there and the machine is
            otherwise healthy, compare it against another unit of the same model before ordering
            parts. If the motor runs but the belt barely moves at all, the adjacent guide is
            <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">turns on but the belt won't move</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-sensor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Speed Sensor: When the Controller Is Misinformed</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills measure belt speed with a small sensor near the front roller or
            motor — commonly a reed switch or optical sensor triggered by a magnet or a marked disc
            as it rotates. The controller counts those pulses and adjusts motor output to hold the
            speed you selected. It is a feedback loop, and it is only as good as the information it
            receives.
          </p>
          <p>
            When the sensor is dirty, loose, or its magnet has shifted, the pulses arrive
            irregularly. The controller reads a missed pulse as the belt slowing, pushes more power
            to correct it, then reads the next pulse as the belt being too fast and backs off. The
            result is hunting: a speed that drifts up and down around the target, or an
            occasional lurch when the controller over-corrects. Erratic distance readings and a
            console speed that does not match how hard the pace feels often come with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some machines respond to a lost sensor signal by stopping and showing an error code,
            which is the safer failure. Others keep trying, which is where surging comes from.
            Either way, this is the most frequent single cause of a speed that fluctuates on its
            own, and it is sometimes a minor one.
          </p>
          <p>
            What an owner can reasonably do, with the machine unplugged and out of warranty, is look
            at the sensor and its magnet with the hood off and remove dust and lint from the sensor
            face. Anything beyond that — re-seating a magnet, adjusting the sensor gap, replacing
            the sensor — is a service job, and on a machine still in warranty the hood should not
            come off at all. Even if cleaning appears to cure it, a surging machine should be
            checked before anyone runs on it again.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-brushes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Worn Motor Brushes</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills use a brushed DC motor. Inside it, two carbon brushes press against
            a rotating commutator to carry current into the motor's windings. The brushes are a
            wearing part by design: they shorten over hundreds of hours of use until they no longer
            make firm, consistent contact.
          </p>
          <p>
            When contact becomes intermittent, the motor receives power in uneven bursts. The
            symptoms are a speed that stumbles or pulses even on a lubricated, correctly tensioned
            belt, more noticeable under load and at low speeds, sometimes with visible sparking
            through the motor's vents or a faint smell. A badly worn or glazed commutator produces
            the same thing and often explains why new brushes alone did not cure it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushes are an inexpensive part on many motors, and on some designs they sit behind
            screw caps on the motor body. That makes it tempting to treat this as a quick owner
            job, and on an out-of-warranty machine with a clear manual procedure, some owners do.
            The honest caveats are that the brush grade and size must match the motor, the
            commutator condition needs assessing at the same time, and any sparking or burning smell
            puts the machine back in stop-and-call territory. The wider picture of motor wear is
            in our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmill motors last</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushless and AC motors, used on some premium and commercial machines, do not have
            brushes at all, so on those this section does not apply and a surge points more
            firmly at the sensor or controller.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wiring-controller" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Loose Wiring, Weak Power and the Controller Board</h2>
<p>
            If the belt, rollers, deck, sensor and brushes are all ruled out, what remains is the
            path the commands and the power take.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The cable at the upright.</strong> The console talks to the controller board
            through a cable that runs down one of the uprights. On folding machines it flexes at
            every fold, and a connector that has worked partly loose can drop the speed command for
            an instant. The signature is a stutter that coincides with leaning on the console,
            gripping the handrails hard, or the frame flexing. Most manuals show where that
            connector sits; reseating it is reasonable once the machine is unplugged.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power supply sag.</strong> A treadmill draws a substantial current, and an
            extension lead, a thin cable or a circuit shared with something heavy can let the
            voltage dip under load. The controller may respond by cutting output momentarily,
            which feels like a stutter, or by shutting down entirely, which is covered in
            <a href="/treadmill-shuts-off-by-itself/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill shuts off by itself</a>.
            Plugging directly into a wall outlet on an unshared circuit costs nothing and rules it
            out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The controller board.</strong> A board with a failing component, or one that has
            been running hot for years under lint and a dry deck, can lose its ability to regulate
            the motor smoothly. This is a diagnosis for a technician with a meter, not for an owner,
            and capacitors on the board can hold a charge after unplugging. If a technician
            confirms the board, the repair-or-replace arithmetic in our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill lifespan guide</a>
            applies: on a mid-range machine several years old, a board can cost a meaningful
            fraction of a better new treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-you-can-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. What You Can Safely Check Yourself</h2>
<p>
            Putting the above into an order of work, from free and external to inside the hood.
            Do these unplugged unless the step says otherwise, and stop at any point where the
            machine surges or smells hot.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Run the tape test with the belt empty, at the lowest speed, from the side rails.</li>
            <li>Try the same speed in manual mode to rule out a programme changing it.</li>
            <li>Plug directly into a wall outlet on its own circuit — no extension lead.</li>
            <li>Inspect the belt top and underside at the point the tape identified.</li>
            <li>Check the deck under the middle of the belt for a dry, grippy surface.</li>
            <li>Do the two-to-three-inch lift test before touching tension.</li>
            <li>Look along the roller ends for wobble with the belt running empty.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Beyond that line — drive belt, sensor, brushes, wiring inside the hood and the board —
            the right move depends on warranty status. Opening the motor hood commonly voids cover,
            and a fault that is covered is one you should not be paying to fix. Parts warranties on
            home machines often run one to three years, so check before picking up a screwdriver.
          </p>
          <p>
            The quarterly routine in our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance guide</a>
            — lubrication, tension check, vacuuming round the motor — prevents most of the
            mechanical causes on this page. It does nothing for a failing sensor or board, which is
            why surging gets treated differently.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-you-call" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. What to Note Before You Call Support</h2>
<p>
            A clear description shortens a support call considerably and makes a misdiagnosis less
            likely. Before you ring, have these to hand.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The model and serial number.</strong> The serial is usually on a sticker near
            the front of the frame, by the motor hood or the power inlet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which rhythm it is.</strong> Once per belt revolution, with each footstrike, a
            fast shudder that rises with speed, or surges with no pattern. Say whether it happens
            with the belt running empty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When it happens.</strong> From cold or only after twenty minutes; at every speed
            or only at the top or bottom of the range; only on incline; only in programmes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What else is going on.</strong> Any error code (note it before power cycling,
            since many clear), any smell, any noise and where it comes from, and whether the machine
            is on an extension lead or shared circuit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What you have already done.</strong> Lubrication date, any tension changes and
            by how much. A technician who knows the belt has been tightened two full turns will look
            at it very differently.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the repair quote lands on a motor or a board and the machine is getting on, it is
            worth reading our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills</a>
            before paying it — a replacement with more motor headroom often avoids the conditions
            that wore out the last one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">13. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my treadmill belt jerking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Time it. A jerk once per belt revolution, with or without you on it, is the belt or a roller — a stiff seam, damage, or debris underneath. A stutter with each footstrike is usually a dry deck or slack belt. Speed that changes on its own is electrical — the speed sensor, controller, motor brushes or a loose connection — and means stop using the machine until it is checked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill belt stutter when I walk on it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A stutter that lands with your steps and disappears with the belt running empty is the belt hesitating under load. The commonest cause is a dry deck raising friction, followed by a genuinely slack belt and a motor that is hot or undersized. Lubricate first, then check tension with the two-to-three-inch lift test before tightening anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill speed fluctuate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                First rule out a programme changing speed by testing in manual mode. If it still drifts, the usual cause is the speed sensor — a dirty sensor or shifted magnet makes the controller over-correct — followed by worn motor brushes, a loose console cable or a failing controller board. Power sag from an extension lead can also cause it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a treadmill that surges?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Speed that surges on its own is one of the symptoms that means stop immediately rather than troubleshoot, because an unexpected change of speed is how people are thrown off the back of a treadmill. Stop the belt, remove the safety key, unplug it, and contact the manufacturer or a technician before anyone uses it again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dry deck make a treadmill belt jerk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the most common cause of a jerk that happens with each footstrike. As lubricant depletes, friction rises until your weight makes the belt hesitate on the front roller and then catch up. Lubricating with 100% silicone, unless your manual specifies otherwise, resolves it — and tightening the belt usually makes it worse.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my treadmill jerk when I change speed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A jerk only at start-up or during speed changes usually points at the drive belt inside the motor hood, which slips under the extra load of acceleration — often with a brief squeal. The rear adjustment bolts do not affect the drive belt, so do not keep tightening the running belt to chase it. That repair is usually a service job.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With a Jerking Belt</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a manageable fault into a bigger one, and the first is a safety issue rather than a cost.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running on a machine that surges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A speed that changes on its own is a controller or sensor fault, and the next surge may come at a pace you cannot absorb. Using the machine at low speed "to see if it does it again" is still using it. Stop, unplug, and get it checked before anyone runs on it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tightening the belt first</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most footstrike stutters are a dry deck, and tightening the belt adds friction and loads the bearings without addressing it. Lubricate, re-test, and only then check tension with the two-to-three-inch lift test. If the jerk is at start-up only, the running-belt bolts are not involved at all.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the tape test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Five minutes with a strip of tape tells you whether the fault travels with the belt, lands with your feet, or does neither. Without it, people replace belts that were fine, tension belts that were dry, and suspect boards when a lump of old lubricant was the whole problem.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening the motor hood under warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sensors, drive belts, brushes and boards sit under the hood, and removing it commonly voids cover. Parts warranties often run one to three years, and plenty of owners pay for repairs they were entitled to. Do the external checks, then call support with your notes.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

